1
00:00:00,112 --> 00:00:01,953
The knock doesn't come again.

2
00:00:01,953 --> 00:00:03,181
It doesn't need to.

3
00:00:03,181 --> 00:00:04,910
You and Mara stand on

4
00:00:04,910 --> 00:00:06,346
either side of the mill door,

5
00:00:06,346 --> 00:00:08,537
as if a door has ever helped you.

6
00:00:08,537 --> 00:00:11,142
Through the crack, you can see it waiting.

7
00:00:11,142 --> 00:00:13,275
It doesn't try the handle.

8
00:00:13,275 --> 00:00:14,300
It doesn't scan.

9
00:00:14,300 --> 00:00:15,917
It just stands there,

10
00:00:15,917 --> 00:00:17,581
in the frost, dripping.

11
00:00:17,581 --> 00:00:20,092
Mara whispers that it walked

12
00:00:20,092 --> 00:00:21,487
through a river to get here.

13
00:00:21,487 --> 00:00:22,497
You whisper that you

14
00:00:22,497 --> 00:00:24,048
don't know what it wants.

15
00:00:24,646 --> 00:00:25,809
She says that never

16
00:00:25,809 --> 00:00:28,032
stopped you letting her in.

17
00:00:28,032 --> 00:00:28,620
Day two.

18
00:00:28,620 --> 00:00:29,881
The shop bell.

19
00:00:29,881 --> 00:00:33,156
Your flashlight, held up in both hands.

20
00:00:33,156 --> 00:00:34,470
You open the door.

21
00:00:34,470 --> 00:00:36,972
The plain figure doesn't come in.

22
00:00:36,972 --> 00:00:38,723
It looks down at the line where the

23
00:00:38,723 --> 00:00:40,684
stone doorstep meets the boards,

24
00:00:40,684 --> 00:00:44,702
and stops there, the way you used to stop at the light.

25
00:00:44,702 --> 00:00:46,701
Then it kneels, and in the white

26
00:00:46,701 --> 00:00:49,194
flour dust that covers everything,

27
00:00:49,194 --> 00:00:51,348
it draws with one gloved finger.

28
00:00:51,802 --> 00:00:54,305
A flat rectangle, with a handle.

29
00:00:54,305 --> 00:00:55,363
Its case.

30
00:00:56,043 --> 00:00:57,460
Then the same shape again,

31
00:00:57,460 --> 00:00:59,492
with a line straight through it.

32
00:01:00,140 --> 00:01:02,209
Then it holds up its empty hands.

33
00:01:02,209 --> 00:01:04,456
You step back from the doorway.

34
00:01:05,120 --> 00:01:07,615
It understands that the way you meant it.

35
00:01:07,615 --> 00:01:09,275
It comes in three steps,

36
00:01:09,275 --> 00:01:11,793
and sits down on the floor by the millstones,

37
00:01:11,793 --> 00:01:14,468
with its back to the wall, facing the door.

38
00:01:14,468 --> 00:01:16,104
It doesn't go any farther.

39
00:01:16,447 --> 00:01:18,377
That night, you lift the last two

40
00:01:18,377 --> 00:01:21,369
planks and stack them inside, out of habit.

41
00:01:21,369 --> 00:01:22,918
It watches you do it.

42
00:01:22,918 --> 00:01:24,568
You lie awake in the loft,

43
00:01:24,568 --> 00:01:26,589
listening to it not sleeping.

44
00:01:27,043 --> 00:01:28,161
On day 122,

45
00:01:28,161 --> 00:01:31,297
you find out it doesn't eat porridge.

46
00:01:31,297 --> 00:01:33,156
Mara offers it a bowl anyway,

47
00:01:33,156 --> 00:01:34,877
because she's Mara.

48
00:01:34,877 --> 00:01:37,228
It looks into the bowl for a long time,

49
00:01:37,228 --> 00:01:38,976
as if it's reading it.

50
00:01:38,976 --> 00:01:40,128
Then it slides it back

51
00:01:40,128 --> 00:01:40,979
across the floor,

52
00:01:40,979 --> 00:01:44,415
very gently, to exactly where she set it down.

53
00:01:44,415 --> 00:01:46,638
From a pocket on its suit,

54
00:01:46,638 --> 00:01:48,192
it takes a small pale cube,

55
00:01:48,192 --> 00:01:50,499
about the size of a sugar lump.

56
00:01:50,499 --> 00:01:52,475
It holds the cube under its chin,

57
00:01:52,475 --> 00:01:54,053
and the cube is gone.

58
00:01:54,428 --> 00:01:55,549
You don't see how.

59
00:01:55,549 --> 00:01:57,620
You decide you don't need to.

60
00:01:58,188 --> 00:01:59,464
Then it does something

61
00:01:59,464 --> 00:02:01,128
you'll think about for weeks.

62
00:02:01,128 --> 00:02:03,928
It opens the pocket and shows it to you.

63
00:02:03,928 --> 00:02:05,221
Four more cubes.

64
00:02:05,580 --> 00:02:07,089
It counts them for you,

65
00:02:07,089 --> 00:02:10,421
one at a time, touching each one with a finger.

66
00:02:10,421 --> 00:02:11,576
You've spent a hundred and

67
00:02:11,576 --> 00:02:14,142
twenty-two days counting your own food.

68
00:02:14,142 --> 00:02:15,110
You never expected

69
00:02:15,110 --> 00:02:17,041
anyone to count theirs for you.

70
00:02:17,382 --> 00:02:19,242
Mara writes it in the ledger,

71
00:02:19,242 --> 00:02:20,332
in a new column.

72
00:02:20,332 --> 00:02:21,841
Its food: four.

73
00:02:21,841 --> 00:02:25,233
By the afternoon, it has found work.

74
00:02:25,590 --> 00:02:28,257
It sweeps the flour dust into neat piles.

75
00:02:28,257 --> 00:02:30,185
It lines up your bottles by height.

76
00:02:30,185 --> 00:02:32,708
When you leave the kettle by the hearth,

77
00:02:32,708 --> 00:02:35,322
it moves it exactly one step to the left.

78
00:02:35,322 --> 00:02:38,268
Like the bicycle, on the very first night.

79
00:02:38,268 --> 00:02:39,581
You move it back.

80
00:02:39,923 --> 00:02:41,336
It looks at the kettle.

81
00:02:41,336 --> 00:02:42,337
It looks at you.

82
00:02:42,337 --> 00:02:43,555
It leaves it there.

83
00:02:43,555 --> 00:02:45,058
Mara says it's the first

84
00:02:45,058 --> 00:02:46,982
argument it has ever lost.

85
00:02:47,292 --> 00:02:48,210
That evening,

86
00:02:48,210 --> 00:02:50,242
you count your own food again.

87
00:02:50,242 --> 00:02:51,737
Eleven potatoes.

88
00:02:51,737 --> 00:02:52,455
One onion.

89
00:02:52,455 --> 00:02:54,213
Eight bags of oats.

90
00:02:54,213 --> 00:02:55,138
Four of flour.

91
00:02:55,138 --> 00:02:57,344
Three of you on an island,

92
00:02:57,344 --> 00:02:59,561
and only two of you eat potatoes.

93
00:02:59,561 --> 00:03:02,894
For once, the arithmetic is on your side.

94
00:03:02,894 --> 00:03:05,773
On day 124, you give it the pencil.

95
00:03:05,773 --> 00:03:08,410
Its own thin drawing tool was in the case.

96
00:03:08,410 --> 00:03:10,361
Mara unrolls the ledger on the

97
00:03:10,361 --> 00:03:12,911
floor and puts the pencil in its glove.

98
00:03:12,911 --> 00:03:15,022
It holds it the wrong way,

99
00:03:15,022 --> 00:03:18,182
then the other wrong way, then works it out.

100
00:03:18,182 --> 00:03:19,396
It draws slowly.

101
00:03:19,396 --> 00:03:22,182
Much slower than the map on day 69.

102
00:03:22,182 --> 00:03:25,272
A hill, with a ring of stones on top.

103
00:03:25,272 --> 00:03:28,164
Small footprints running down the slope.

104
00:03:28,491 --> 00:03:30,234
A figure kneeling beside them,

105
00:03:30,234 --> 00:03:32,165
one hand flat on the ground.

106
00:03:32,620 --> 00:03:33,360
Itself.

107
00:03:33,360 --> 00:03:35,603
Next, the same hill,

108
00:03:35,978 --> 00:03:39,273
and a second figure, with a stripe across its shoulder.

109
00:03:39,273 --> 00:03:40,595
The striped figure is

110
00:03:40,595 --> 00:03:41,974
holding the flat case.

111
00:03:41,974 --> 00:03:43,433
The kneeling one isn't

112
00:03:43,433 --> 00:03:45,417
holding anything anymore.

113
00:03:45,417 --> 00:03:47,530
Last, a long line of water,

114
00:03:47,530 --> 00:03:51,530
and one small figure standing in it, up to its chest.

115
00:03:51,530 --> 00:03:53,437
It puts the pencil down.

116
00:03:53,957 --> 00:03:55,985
Nobody says anything for a while.

117
00:03:55,985 --> 00:03:58,261
You saw it on day 94, from the wall.

118
00:03:58,261 --> 00:04:00,251
You thought it was a kindness.

119
00:04:00,251 --> 00:04:01,804
You never thought

120
00:04:01,804 --> 00:04:03,293
about who else was watching.

121
00:04:03,293 --> 00:04:04,422
Mara says it quietly,

122
00:04:04,422 --> 00:04:07,121
the way you'd say it about a person.

123
00:04:07,121 --> 00:04:08,666
It lost its job for us.

124
00:04:08,666 --> 00:04:11,142
You think about it a little longer.

125
00:04:11,142 --> 00:04:12,115
It didn't lose it.

126
00:04:12,115 --> 00:04:12,832
It traded it.

127
00:04:12,832 --> 00:04:14,992
Out on the horizon, there's a ship that

128
00:04:14,992 --> 00:04:17,492
knows exactly what this figure did for you.

129
00:04:17,492 --> 00:04:18,807
And here on an island,

130
00:04:18,807 --> 00:04:20,741
that figure is sitting on a cold

131
00:04:20,741 --> 00:04:22,849
floor with nothing in its hands.

132
00:04:22,849 --> 00:04:23,615
That night,

133
00:04:23,615 --> 00:04:26,502
Mara takes an empty flour sack and lays it on

134
00:04:26,502 --> 00:04:28,236
the floor by the millstones.

135
00:04:28,236 --> 00:04:30,511
It looks at the sack.

136
00:04:30,511 --> 00:04:32,413
Then it sits on it.

137
00:04:32,413 --> 00:04:34,539
On day 127, it snows.

138
00:04:34,539 --> 00:04:38,106
You wake to a silence so thick it's as

139
00:04:38,106 --> 00:04:40,432
if someone has wrapped the mill in cloth.

140
00:04:40,432 --> 00:04:41,784
The weir is white.

141
00:04:42,094 --> 00:04:43,483
The banks are white.

142
00:04:43,483 --> 00:04:45,735
The big wheel has a line of snow

143
00:04:45,735 --> 00:04:48,781
along every paddle, and it still turns.

144
00:04:49,171 --> 00:04:51,730
Snow is beautiful for about a minute.

145
00:04:51,730 --> 00:04:52,875
Then you realize

146
00:04:52,875 --> 00:04:55,227
everything you do now will show.

147
00:04:55,227 --> 00:04:57,107
Smoke on a white sky.

148
00:04:57,592 --> 00:04:59,571
Footprints on a white walkway.

149
00:04:59,571 --> 00:05:01,531
Mara counts the firewood.

150
00:05:02,049 --> 00:05:02,780
Old crates.

151
00:05:02,780 --> 00:05:05,090
Broken teeth from a wooden gear.

152
00:05:05,511 --> 00:05:08,184
A ladder to a loft you don't use.

153
00:05:08,184 --> 00:05:09,313
Two weeks,

154
00:05:09,313 --> 00:05:12,150
maybe, if you're careful, and you're always careful.

155
00:05:12,150 --> 00:05:14,213
At dusk, you take the boat along

156
00:05:14,213 --> 00:05:15,846
the edge of the river and pull dead

157
00:05:15,846 --> 00:05:18,318
branches out of the snow on the bank.

158
00:05:18,318 --> 00:05:19,450
When you come back,

159
00:05:19,450 --> 00:05:22,094
the plain figure is standing at the window,

160
00:05:22,094 --> 00:05:24,976
very still, looking at the far bank.

161
00:05:24,976 --> 00:05:25,779
You look too.

162
00:05:25,779 --> 00:05:28,187
In the fresh snow along the river,

163
00:05:28,187 --> 00:05:30,431
there's a line of footprints.

164
00:05:30,431 --> 00:05:30,880
Long.

165
00:05:30,880 --> 00:05:31,778
Narrow.

166
00:05:32,121 --> 00:05:32,811
Not human.

167
00:05:32,811 --> 00:05:34,129
They go up the bank,

168
00:05:34,129 --> 00:05:36,536
and down the bank, and up again,

169
00:05:36,536 --> 00:05:39,152
as if someone walked back and forth all afternoon,

170
00:05:39,152 --> 00:05:41,047
looking across the water.

171
00:05:41,047 --> 00:05:41,992
The plain figure

172
00:05:41,992 --> 00:05:43,548
steps away from the window.

173
00:05:43,548 --> 00:05:45,450
It sits down on its sack,

174
00:05:45,450 --> 00:05:48,110
facing the door, and doesn't move again until morning.

175
00:05:48,110 --> 00:05:50,360
You've seen people hide before.

176
00:05:50,360 --> 00:05:51,910
You've done it for a

177
00:05:51,910 --> 00:05:52,652
hundred and twenty-seven days.

178
00:05:52,652 --> 00:05:53,748
You never thought

179
00:05:53,748 --> 00:05:55,814
you'd see one of them do it.

180
00:05:56,204 --> 00:05:56,878
Mara asks it,

181
00:05:56,878 --> 00:05:59,250
with her hands, who walked on that bank.

182
00:05:59,833 --> 00:06:01,370
It doesn't draw anything.

183
00:06:01,370 --> 00:06:03,321
It just touches its own shoulder,

184
00:06:03,321 --> 00:06:06,047
where the other one has a stripe.

185
00:06:06,047 --> 00:06:07,605
On day 130,

186
00:06:07,605 --> 00:06:09,784
you ask your question again.

187
00:06:09,784 --> 00:06:11,662
The one from day 69.

188
00:06:12,100 --> 00:06:13,723
You draw the stadium bowl,

189
00:06:13,723 --> 00:06:16,956
little figures walking in, and a question mark

190
00:06:16,956 --> 00:06:19,151
big enough that nobody could miss it.

191
00:06:19,151 --> 00:06:20,123
This time,

192
00:06:20,123 --> 00:06:23,055
you hand it straight to the one who can answer.

193
00:06:23,055 --> 00:06:24,467
It looks at the

194
00:06:24,467 --> 00:06:26,070
question mark for a long time.

195
00:06:26,070 --> 00:06:27,146
Then it draws.

196
00:06:27,600 --> 00:06:28,548
The stadium.

197
00:06:28,548 --> 00:06:30,297
The mast with its ring.

198
00:06:30,297 --> 00:06:32,301
The park by the river.

199
00:06:32,301 --> 00:06:33,633
Pairs of tiny figures

200
00:06:33,633 --> 00:06:35,311
walking into each one.

201
00:06:35,311 --> 00:06:36,835
A small craft,

202
00:06:36,835 --> 00:06:39,642
an arrow, and then the ship,

203
00:06:39,642 --> 00:06:43,062
a long flat shape across the top of the paper.

204
00:06:43,062 --> 00:06:45,918
Inside the ship, it draws rows.

205
00:06:46,276 --> 00:06:49,421
Rows and rows of tiny joined circles,

206
00:06:49,421 --> 00:06:52,658
packed side by side, like seats in a theater.

207
00:06:53,176 --> 00:06:54,625
It keeps drawing until the

208
00:06:54,625 --> 00:06:56,127
ship is almost full of them,

209
00:06:56,127 --> 00:06:58,986
and your arm aches just from watching.

210
00:06:58,986 --> 00:07:01,674
Then it draws a line from the ship,

211
00:07:01,674 --> 00:07:04,404
curving up and away, off the top of the page.

212
00:07:04,730 --> 00:07:07,425
Mara looks at the rows of circles.

213
00:07:07,425 --> 00:07:09,837
On day 42, she said collecting.

214
00:07:09,837 --> 00:07:11,167
You said counting.

215
00:07:11,167 --> 00:07:12,937
You were both right.

216
00:07:13,407 --> 00:07:14,810
Every pair that follows

217
00:07:14,810 --> 00:07:16,212
the lights gets a seat.

218
00:07:16,212 --> 00:07:18,020
Every seat gets counted.

219
00:07:18,020 --> 00:07:19,398
And when the seats are full,

220
00:07:19,398 --> 00:07:20,411
the ship goes.

221
00:07:20,929 --> 00:07:21,276
Up.

222
00:07:21,276 --> 00:07:21,798
Away.

223
00:07:21,798 --> 00:07:24,617
Off the edge of the page.

224
00:07:24,617 --> 00:07:25,133
You ask,

225
00:07:25,133 --> 00:07:27,138
with your hands, what's out there.

226
00:07:27,138 --> 00:07:29,488
It looks at the edge of the paper.

227
00:07:29,488 --> 00:07:31,004
Then it puts the pencil down.

228
00:07:31,004 --> 00:07:32,296
Either it doesn't know.

229
00:07:32,296 --> 00:07:34,451
Or it doesn't have a drawing for it.

230
00:07:34,451 --> 00:07:36,340
You write in the ledger:

231
00:07:36,340 --> 00:07:37,529
they take them away.

232
00:07:37,529 --> 00:07:39,415
Neither of you adds theory.

233
00:07:39,788 --> 00:07:43,264
On day 133, it draws the ship again.

234
00:07:43,621 --> 00:07:46,009
This time, every seat is filled in.

235
00:07:46,009 --> 00:07:49,322
Every pair of circles, dark with pencil.

236
00:07:49,322 --> 00:07:50,226
Except one.

237
00:07:50,226 --> 00:07:52,854
At the very end of the last row,

238
00:07:52,854 --> 00:07:54,210
there's a single circle.

239
00:07:54,210 --> 00:07:54,890
Alone.

240
00:07:55,408 --> 00:07:55,906
Empty.

241
00:07:55,906 --> 00:07:57,469
You know that circle.

242
00:07:57,469 --> 00:08:00,597
You saw it glowing on a hilltop on day 90,

243
00:08:00,597 --> 00:08:03,219
beside a gap only wide enough for one.

244
00:08:03,219 --> 00:08:04,198
It was a seat.

245
00:08:04,198 --> 00:08:05,176
The last seat.

246
00:08:05,176 --> 00:08:07,431
And they offered it to one of you.

247
00:08:07,431 --> 00:08:09,266
Then the plain figure draws a

248
00:08:09,266 --> 00:08:11,449
row of short lines under the ship.

249
00:08:11,449 --> 00:08:12,070
One line,

250
00:08:12,070 --> 00:08:15,664
then another, the way you used to count patrols.

251
00:08:15,664 --> 00:08:17,448
It draws for a long time.

252
00:08:17,448 --> 00:08:21,021
When it stops, Mara counts them out loud.

253
00:08:21,021 --> 00:08:21,321
Forty-seven.

254
00:08:21,321 --> 00:08:23,492
It points at the first line,

255
00:08:23,492 --> 00:08:26,211
then at the window, at today.

256
00:08:26,211 --> 00:08:28,062
Then at the last line,

257
00:08:28,062 --> 00:08:29,878
and at the ship.

258
00:08:29,878 --> 00:08:31,904
And it draws an arrow, up.

259
00:08:31,904 --> 00:08:34,226
You do the math on the back

260
00:08:34,226 --> 00:08:36,398
of the map, the slow way, twice.

261
00:08:36,932 --> 00:08:40,191
Day 133, plus forty-seven.

262
00:08:40,847 --> 00:08:42,422
Day 180.

263
00:08:42,422 --> 00:08:44,954
In forty-seven days,

264
00:08:44,954 --> 00:08:48,070
the ship leaves, full or not.

265
00:08:48,070 --> 00:08:50,822
Mara asks the question you can't.

266
00:08:50,822 --> 00:08:52,891
What happens to the ones who stay?

267
00:08:53,377 --> 00:08:55,430
The plain figure looks at her.

268
00:08:55,430 --> 00:08:57,073
Then, very small,

269
00:08:57,073 --> 00:08:59,901
at the bottom of the page, it draws the mill.

270
00:09:00,293 --> 00:09:02,111
Two figures beside it.

271
00:09:02,111 --> 00:09:04,647
And a third, with no case.

272
00:09:04,647 --> 00:09:05,694
It isn't going.

273
00:09:05,694 --> 00:09:08,518
Without its case, maybe it can't.

274
00:09:08,518 --> 00:09:10,187
At the top of the roll,

275
00:09:10,187 --> 00:09:13,963
you write the number big, and draw a box around it.

276
00:09:16,207 --> 00:09:16,926
180.

277
00:09:16,926 --> 00:09:18,652
On day 136,

278
00:09:18,652 --> 00:09:21,493
all three of you go for supplies.

279
00:09:21,493 --> 00:09:23,520
The oats won't last the winter,

280
00:09:23,520 --> 00:09:25,148
and neither will the wood.

281
00:09:25,148 --> 00:09:26,836
You row downstream at dusk,

282
00:09:26,836 --> 00:09:31,056
the plain figure sitting very still in the middle of the boat,

283
00:09:31,056 --> 00:09:33,488
as if it has never been in one.

284
00:09:33,488 --> 00:09:34,967
It probably hasn't.

285
00:09:35,325 --> 00:09:38,405
The can bobs along behind you on its line.

286
00:09:38,405 --> 00:09:41,150
An hour downriver, there's a village.

287
00:09:41,150 --> 00:09:43,517
A church, a row of houses,

288
00:09:43,517 --> 00:09:46,038
and a little shop with its door kicked in.

289
00:09:46,038 --> 00:09:47,516
On the main street,

290
00:09:47,516 --> 00:09:50,541
units stand at intervals, their circles dark.

291
00:09:51,059 --> 00:09:52,752
When you step onto the road,

292
00:09:52,752 --> 00:09:54,666
the nearest pair glows.

293
00:09:54,666 --> 00:09:55,415
Then the next.

294
00:09:55,415 --> 00:09:56,514
The lights still want

295
00:09:56,514 --> 00:09:57,963
to take you somewhere.

296
00:09:58,417 --> 00:10:00,441
The plain figure stays behind you,

297
00:10:00,441 --> 00:10:03,250
away from the units, close to the walls.

298
00:10:03,250 --> 00:10:05,546
It's hiding from its own lights.

299
00:10:05,951 --> 00:10:07,427
On almost every front door,

300
00:10:07,427 --> 00:10:10,818
at about the height of a hand, there's a small mark.

301
00:10:10,818 --> 00:10:13,289
Two circles, joined by a short line.

302
00:10:13,663 --> 00:10:16,046
The houses behind those doors are empty.

303
00:10:16,046 --> 00:10:18,649
Every pair that lived here has a seat now.

304
00:10:18,649 --> 00:10:20,582
The shop has been picked over,

305
00:10:20,582 --> 00:10:22,323
but not by anyone thorough.

306
00:10:22,323 --> 00:10:25,010
In a back room, you find a box of tins.

307
00:10:25,384 --> 00:10:25,701
Fourteen.

308
00:10:25,701 --> 00:10:27,128
A packet of salt.

309
00:10:27,128 --> 00:10:30,266
Two wool blankets, still in their plastic.

310
00:10:30,266 --> 00:10:31,031
A hand saw.

311
00:10:31,031 --> 00:10:33,279
And a box of white candles.

312
00:10:33,670 --> 00:10:33,965
Twelve.

313
00:10:33,965 --> 00:10:36,253
Light, for the first time since

314
00:10:36,253 --> 00:10:38,379
the lantern went into the lock.

315
00:10:38,379 --> 00:10:41,855
You load everything and walk back to the boat fast,

316
00:10:41,855 --> 00:10:43,821
along the backs of the houses.

317
00:10:43,821 --> 00:10:45,793
Behind you, the circles on the

318
00:10:45,793 --> 00:10:47,106
main street light up,

319
00:10:47,106 --> 00:10:50,919
pair after pair, all the way to the end of the village,

320
00:10:50,919 --> 00:10:52,467
where nobody is walking.

321
00:10:53,082 --> 00:10:53,949
That night,

322
00:10:53,949 --> 00:10:56,406
you light one candle in the loft.

323
00:10:56,406 --> 00:10:58,879
Just one, just for a minute.

324
00:10:58,879 --> 00:11:00,644
The plain figure watches the

325
00:11:00,644 --> 00:11:02,843
flame the way you once watched

326
00:11:02,843 --> 00:11:04,536
the first lit circle on the road.

327
00:11:04,536 --> 00:11:06,002
Then Mara blows it out

328
00:11:06,002 --> 00:11:08,485
and writes down the number.

329
00:11:08,485 --> 00:11:08,839
Eleven.

330
00:11:08,839 --> 00:11:10,144
On day 139,

331
00:11:10,144 --> 00:11:13,019
the plain figure is slower.

332
00:11:13,474 --> 00:11:15,136
It takes longer to stand.

333
00:11:15,136 --> 00:11:18,105
It sits closer to the fire than it used to,

334
00:11:18,105 --> 00:11:21,421
and its pale green face has gone a shade grayer,

335
00:11:21,421 --> 00:11:23,808
like a leaf at the end of autumn.

336
00:11:23,808 --> 00:11:25,079
It shows you the

337
00:11:25,079 --> 00:11:27,147
pocket without being asked.

338
00:11:27,147 --> 00:11:28,406
Two cubes.

339
00:11:28,406 --> 00:11:30,652
You find the column in the ledger.

340
00:11:30,652 --> 00:11:31,771
Its food: four.

341
00:11:32,240 --> 00:11:34,179
That was seventeen days ago.

342
00:11:34,179 --> 00:11:35,622
You do the math,

343
00:11:35,622 --> 00:11:37,434
because that's who you are now.

344
00:11:37,434 --> 00:11:38,227
At this rate,

345
00:11:38,227 --> 00:11:41,812
the cubes run out before day 160.

346
00:11:41,812 --> 00:11:45,114
Day 180 is twenty days after that.

347
00:11:45,114 --> 00:11:47,083
Mara tries again.

348
00:11:47,083 --> 00:11:50,331
Porridge, potato, a corner of tinned fish.

349
00:11:50,331 --> 00:11:52,672
It looks at each one politely,

350
00:11:52,672 --> 00:11:53,778
and slides it back.

351
00:11:53,778 --> 00:11:55,105
Whatever keeps it going

352
00:11:55,105 --> 00:11:56,692
doesn't grow on this planet.

353
00:11:56,692 --> 00:11:59,511
So you give it the only thing you've got.

354
00:11:59,511 --> 00:12:00,721
The warmest place.

355
00:12:00,721 --> 00:12:03,013
You move its sack beside the hearth,

356
00:12:03,013 --> 00:12:04,576
and Mara folds one of the

357
00:12:04,576 --> 00:12:06,529
new blankets around its shoulders.

358
00:12:06,529 --> 00:12:07,973
It sits there,

359
00:12:07,973 --> 00:12:10,461
wrapped up, staring into the fire.

360
00:12:10,461 --> 00:12:11,291
On day one,

361
00:12:11,291 --> 00:12:14,012
you watched these figures step out of a craft,

362
00:12:14,012 --> 00:12:15,910
and you were terrified of them.

363
00:12:15,910 --> 00:12:17,679
You still are, a little.

364
00:12:18,214 --> 00:12:20,199
But this one is sitting by your fire,

365
00:12:20,199 --> 00:12:21,746
running out of food,

366
00:12:21,746 --> 00:12:23,633
because it wiped away your footprints.

367
00:12:23,633 --> 00:12:25,777
You don't know how to count that.

368
00:12:25,777 --> 00:12:28,325
So you count everything else.

369
00:12:28,325 --> 00:12:29,644
Seven potatoes.

370
00:12:29,955 --> 00:12:31,534
Six bags of oats.

371
00:12:31,534 --> 00:12:33,018
Four of flour.

372
00:12:33,329 --> 00:12:34,447
Twelve tins.

373
00:12:34,447 --> 00:12:35,938
Ten candles.

374
00:12:35,938 --> 00:12:37,356
Two cubes.

375
00:12:37,811 --> 00:12:41,710
On day 142, you wake to slow footsteps.

376
00:12:42,053 --> 00:12:43,469
They aren't on the island.

377
00:12:43,469 --> 00:12:44,780
They're on the far bank.

378
00:12:44,780 --> 00:12:46,129
Then the near bank.

379
00:12:46,552 --> 00:12:47,516
Back and forth,

380
00:12:47,516 --> 00:12:50,001
all night, crunching in the snow.

381
00:12:50,342 --> 00:12:52,222
In the morning, you look out.

382
00:12:52,222 --> 00:12:55,341
A band of pale light runs along the far bank,

383
00:12:55,341 --> 00:12:56,924
right at the edge of the water,

384
00:12:56,924 --> 00:12:59,555
as far as you can see in both directions.

385
00:12:59,555 --> 00:13:01,602
Another runs along the near bank.

386
00:13:01,959 --> 00:13:03,484
Two long lines of light,

387
00:13:03,484 --> 00:13:05,253
with the river between them.

388
00:13:05,253 --> 00:13:06,812
And you in the middle.

389
00:13:06,812 --> 00:13:08,653
It's the hallway from the lock,

390
00:13:08,653 --> 00:13:11,304
drawn along a river instead of up a hill.

391
00:13:11,304 --> 00:13:14,070
And it still doesn't touch the water.

392
00:13:14,070 --> 00:13:15,025
On the far bank,

393
00:13:15,025 --> 00:13:17,178
a figure stands in the snow.

394
00:13:17,178 --> 00:13:19,042
A blue stripe on its shoulder.

395
00:13:19,042 --> 00:13:20,905
In one hand, the scanner.

396
00:13:21,360 --> 00:13:23,727
In the other, a flat silver case.

397
00:13:23,727 --> 00:13:26,111
The plain figure sees it too.

398
00:13:26,111 --> 00:13:28,911
It stands at the window for a long time.

399
00:13:28,911 --> 00:13:30,447
Then it takes the pencil,

400
00:13:30,447 --> 00:13:31,492
and on the roll,

401
00:13:31,492 --> 00:13:34,533
it draws two circles, joined by a short line.

402
00:13:34,533 --> 00:13:36,142
One with a stripe across it.

403
00:13:36,142 --> 00:13:37,164
One without.

404
00:13:37,507 --> 00:13:38,970
Mara looks at the drawing.

405
00:13:38,970 --> 00:13:41,004
Then at the figure in the snow.

406
00:13:41,427 --> 00:13:43,823
You've seen joined circles on roads,

407
00:13:43,823 --> 00:13:46,337
on rails, at the base of every unit.

408
00:13:46,337 --> 00:13:47,772
You never once asked

409
00:13:47,772 --> 00:13:49,832
who drew the first pair.

410
00:13:49,832 --> 00:13:52,182
Or what it meant to the ones who drew it.

411
00:13:52,182 --> 00:13:54,421
The striped figure stands on the

412
00:13:54,421 --> 00:13:56,111
bank all day, looking at the mill.

413
00:13:56,111 --> 00:13:57,477
It doesn't cross.

414
00:13:57,477 --> 00:13:58,895
By dusk, it's gone.

415
00:13:58,895 --> 00:14:02,123
Its footprints lead away through the snow,

416
00:14:02,123 --> 00:14:04,654
and then, a little later, they lead back.

417
00:14:05,252 --> 00:14:08,202
On day 145, the cold gets serious.

418
00:14:08,202 --> 00:14:11,011
Your breath freezes on the blankets.

419
00:14:11,011 --> 00:14:12,533
The candles are too precious,

420
00:14:12,533 --> 00:14:14,289
so the nights are long and dark,

421
00:14:14,289 --> 00:14:16,271
and the fire is the whole world.

422
00:14:16,271 --> 00:14:19,103
You cut the wood smaller with the hand saw,

423
00:14:19,103 --> 00:14:21,553
so it lasts longer, and so do your arms.

424
00:14:21,910 --> 00:14:23,274
You eat the tins cold,

425
00:14:23,274 --> 00:14:24,993
to save the wood for warmth.

426
00:14:24,993 --> 00:14:26,797
Every morning, there's a strip of

427
00:14:26,797 --> 00:14:28,640
ice along the edges of the river.

428
00:14:28,982 --> 00:14:29,638
Thin, gray,

429
00:14:29,638 --> 00:14:32,344
and a little wider than the morning before.

430
00:14:32,750 --> 00:14:35,252
That afternoon, Mara notices it first.

431
00:14:35,252 --> 00:14:37,011
The band on the near

432
00:14:37,011 --> 00:14:38,175
bank isn't where it was.

433
00:14:38,175 --> 00:14:38,641
Yesterday,

434
00:14:38,641 --> 00:14:40,691
it stopped where the grass ended.

435
00:14:40,691 --> 00:14:43,401
Today, it stops where the ice ends.

436
00:14:43,401 --> 00:14:46,060
A hand's width out over the river.

437
00:14:46,060 --> 00:14:46,945
On the ice.

438
00:14:46,945 --> 00:14:49,152
You sit on the cold stone of

439
00:14:49,152 --> 00:14:50,696
the weir and watch it all day.

440
00:14:50,696 --> 00:14:52,075
Every time the ice grows,

441
00:14:52,075 --> 00:14:53,518
the band is already on it.

442
00:14:53,518 --> 00:14:54,693
You thought the light

443
00:14:54,693 --> 00:14:56,249
couldn't touch the water.

444
00:14:56,249 --> 00:14:58,878
Maybe it never cared about water at all.

445
00:14:59,220 --> 00:15:00,486
Maybe it just can't hold

446
00:15:00,486 --> 00:15:02,154
onto anything that moves.

447
00:15:02,512 --> 00:15:03,661
You write it down,

448
00:15:03,661 --> 00:15:07,107
and out of habit, you write observed next to it.

449
00:15:07,107 --> 00:15:08,980
Then you cross observed out,

450
00:15:08,980 --> 00:15:11,032
and write: it's coming.

451
00:15:11,032 --> 00:15:13,756
The plain figure watches the ice with you.

452
00:15:13,756 --> 00:15:14,516
On the roll,

453
00:15:14,516 --> 00:15:16,728
it draws a circle around the mill.

454
00:15:16,728 --> 00:15:18,858
Then the same circle, smaller.

455
00:15:18,858 --> 00:15:20,382
Then smaller again.

456
00:15:20,382 --> 00:15:23,626
You don't need it to draw a fourth.

457
00:15:23,626 --> 00:15:26,338
On day 148, the wheel stops.

458
00:15:26,338 --> 00:15:28,081
You hear it in your sleep.

459
00:15:28,081 --> 00:15:29,483
For thirty-six nights,

460
00:15:29,483 --> 00:15:32,839
it has creaked below you, slow and steady,

461
00:15:32,839 --> 00:15:35,402
like someone breathing in the next room.

462
00:15:35,402 --> 00:15:38,137
Then, in the middle of the night,

463
00:15:38,137 --> 00:15:38,853
it doesn't.

464
00:15:38,853 --> 00:15:40,107
In the morning,

465
00:15:40,498 --> 00:15:43,880
it's locked in ice, every paddle hung with icicles.

466
00:15:44,239 --> 00:15:46,137
The mill channel is frozen solid.

467
00:15:46,137 --> 00:15:48,766
The river has shrunk to a dark strip of

468
00:15:48,766 --> 00:15:50,003
open water down the middle,

469
00:15:50,003 --> 00:15:53,005
and the bands have followed the ice in from both sides.

470
00:15:53,005 --> 00:15:54,675
You have to decide today.

471
00:15:54,675 --> 00:15:55,594
You can stay,

472
00:15:55,594 --> 00:15:58,811
with walls, a fire, and food for maybe a month.

473
00:15:59,330 --> 00:16:00,715
Or you can take the boat down

474
00:16:00,715 --> 00:16:01,869
that last strip of water,

475
00:16:01,869 --> 00:16:04,110
now, while there's still water to take.

476
00:16:04,110 --> 00:16:05,671
You drag the boat out of the shadow

477
00:16:05,671 --> 00:16:08,115
of the wheel, onto the edge of the ice.

478
00:16:08,115 --> 00:16:09,581
It's heavy with the food,

479
00:16:09,581 --> 00:16:11,438
the blankets, and the can.

480
00:16:11,438 --> 00:16:14,130
The plain figure gets up from the fire.

481
00:16:14,130 --> 00:16:15,838
It takes three tries.

482
00:16:15,838 --> 00:16:18,278
It walks to the boat, and stops.

483
00:16:18,700 --> 00:16:19,618
It can't row.

484
00:16:19,618 --> 00:16:21,295
It can barely stand.

485
00:16:21,812 --> 00:16:23,104
Mara looks at you.

486
00:16:23,104 --> 00:16:25,007
You know what she's asking.

487
00:16:25,007 --> 00:16:27,081
Two, whatever happens.

488
00:16:27,081 --> 00:16:29,118
But there are three of you now.

489
00:16:29,507 --> 00:16:30,777
You pull the boat back

490
00:16:30,777 --> 00:16:32,433
into the shadow of the wheel.

491
00:16:32,433 --> 00:16:34,153
The plain figure sits

492
00:16:34,153 --> 00:16:35,505
down by the fire again.

493
00:16:35,505 --> 00:16:37,202
It holds out its hand,

494
00:16:37,202 --> 00:16:39,463
palm up, and opens it.

495
00:16:39,463 --> 00:16:40,249
One cube.

496
00:16:40,249 --> 00:16:43,203
It has been skipping them.

497
00:16:43,203 --> 00:16:46,281
On day 150, the river is gone.

498
00:16:46,281 --> 00:16:47,863
Not dried up.

499
00:16:47,863 --> 00:16:48,771
Gone white.

500
00:16:48,771 --> 00:16:52,196
From bank to bank, from the weir to the bend,

501
00:16:52,196 --> 00:16:54,687
it's one flat field of ice,

502
00:16:54,687 --> 00:16:57,136
under a sky the color of dishwater.

503
00:16:57,136 --> 00:16:58,782
The last strip of open

504
00:16:58,782 --> 00:17:00,447
water closed in the night.

505
00:17:00,447 --> 00:17:01,619
And the bands have

506
00:17:01,619 --> 00:17:03,862
walked out to meet each other.

507
00:17:03,862 --> 00:17:05,672
They run across the ice from both

508
00:17:05,672 --> 00:17:08,026
banks and join in a ring around the island.

509
00:17:08,026 --> 00:17:09,395
Around the mill.

510
00:17:09,395 --> 00:17:10,246
Around you.

511
00:17:10,246 --> 00:17:12,800
Every way out, by land or by water,

512
00:17:12,800 --> 00:17:14,782
is now the same thing.

513
00:17:14,782 --> 00:17:15,336
A floor.

514
00:17:15,336 --> 00:17:17,114
With light drawn on it.

515
00:17:17,633 --> 00:17:18,554
On the ice,

516
00:17:18,554 --> 00:17:22,350
outside the ring, stands the striped figure.

517
00:17:22,350 --> 00:17:24,490
The silver case is at its feet.

518
00:17:24,490 --> 00:17:26,527
Its scanner is lowered.

519
00:17:26,527 --> 00:17:27,873
The ring isn't whole.

520
00:17:27,873 --> 00:17:31,029
On the side that faces the striped figure,

521
00:17:31,029 --> 00:17:31,991
there's a gap.

522
00:17:31,991 --> 00:17:32,505
Narrow.

523
00:17:32,505 --> 00:17:34,538
Narrower than the station.

524
00:17:34,897 --> 00:17:36,603
Narrower than the store.

525
00:17:36,603 --> 00:17:38,951
Exactly as narrow as a hilltop,

526
00:17:38,951 --> 00:17:40,052
sixty days ago.

527
00:17:40,052 --> 00:17:43,581
Beside it, a single circle begins to glow.

528
00:17:43,581 --> 00:17:45,572
You've seen this before.

529
00:17:45,979 --> 00:17:47,745
You know what comes next.

530
00:17:47,745 --> 00:17:48,967
You look at Mara.

531
00:17:48,967 --> 00:17:50,096
But this time,

532
00:17:50,096 --> 00:17:52,125
she isn't looking at the gap.

533
00:17:52,125 --> 00:17:54,984
She's looking at the plain figure,

534
00:17:54,984 --> 00:17:56,591
wrapped in its blanket,

535
00:17:56,591 --> 00:17:59,781
standing very still in the doorway behind you.

536
00:17:59,781 --> 00:18:01,907
It's only wide enough for one.

537
00:18:01,907 --> 00:18:04,805
And this time, you know who it's for.
